solution.p:
First, we will find Line As slope by rewriting its equation from standard form to slope-intercept form:
\begin{aligned}
2x+3y\amp=12\\
3y\amp=12\subtractright{2x}\\
3y\amp=-2x+12\\
y\amp=\divideunder{-2x+12}{3}\\
y\amp=-\frac{2}{3}x+4
\end{aligned}
So Line As slope is -\frac{2}{3}\text{.} Since Line B is perpendicular to Line A\text{,} its slope is the negative reciprocal, \frac{3}{2}\text{.} Its also given that Line B passes through (4,-3)\text{,} so we can write Line Bs point-slope form equation:
\begin{aligned}
y\amp=m(x-x_0)+y_0\\
y\amp=\frac{3}{2}(x-4)-3
\end{aligned}
